# Memcheck suppressions for the valgrind sweep in `tools/nv/driver/legs.ts` and for `tools/leak-check.sh`.
#
# Two contexts, one cause. `ring`'s AEAD is hand-written AES-NI/PCLMULQDQ assembly that
# operates a whole 16-byte block at a time and masks off the part it must not use.
# Memcheck tracks definedness bit by bit through ordinary instructions and cannot follow
# that masking through the vector ones, so it reports the block's tail as undefined and
# then reports every branch and every syscall that value reaches. Novis never touches
# those bytes: it hands rustls a plaintext and rustls hands the kernel a sealed record.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT HIDE. Both entries are anchored on a frame inside
# `ring` or inside the one rustls function that writes a sealed record to a socket, so an
# uninitialised value that Novis's own unsafe code produced is still an error, including
# one written into a buffer that later reaches `sendto` by any other path. Nothing here
# suppresses a leak of any kind — the sweep's whole subject is the refcount protocol, and
# `--errors-for-leak-kinds=definite` is what scopes that.
#
# If a `ring` upgrade inlines `open_within` into its caller, the first entry stops
# matching and the sweep goes red again. That is the safe direction to fail in: a
# suppression that silently stops covering its case is visible, where one broad enough to
# survive any inlining would be hiding whatever else grew under it.

{
   ring's AEAD open branches on a block tail memcheck cannot see masked off
   Memcheck:Cond
   fun:*4ring4aead*
   ...
}

{
   the sealed record's tag reads as undefined all the way out to sendto
   Memcheck:Param
   socketcall.sendto(msg)
   ...
   fun:*6rustls6vecbuf*ChunkVecBuffer8write_to
   ...
}
